[Music]  today in our school matters series we  are answering questions from parents as  kids head back to school we're going to  go over everything from mask wearing to  helping students manage stress  right now covet cases are surging among  children more than 180 thousand new  cases were reported last week alone and  that is a four-fold increase from just a  month ago  our senior medical correspondent dr tara  narula and psychologist and cbs news  contributor lisa demour join us now for  more good morning to both of you tara  good morning table with us so let's  begin with you we've got a viewer  question from rita marie who would like  to know what is the best mask for  children to wear and what is your advice  for choosing a mask in general for  students well let's just start off by  being clear that both the cdc and the  american academy of pediatrics are  recommending universal masking in  schools so yes for many parents on their  shopping list for supplies this year are  masks the best mask most experts will  say is the one that your child will wear  and the one that fits them well so  getting them involved in that choice  picking out the colors and styles they  like or letting them decorate it is  always a good thing when you're picking  out the type you want to pick one that  has multiple layers of material in  particular at least two layers you can  hold it up to the sun and make sure that  you can't see light through it a  surgical mask is a good option and some  parents may feel more comfortable with  those kid-sized kn 95s although for the  child that that may be uncomfortable  it's probably not necessary when you're  looking for what else to choose you want  to choose one that has a nose wire  because that kind of fits nicely around  their face and the ear loops so you can  adjust it so it fits snuggly you want to  make sure you're washing it frequently  if it's not disposable and definitely  pack more than one because my children i  know have lost theirs or they've dropped  on the floor so  no bandanas no i've noticed something  some parents have put it's almost like a  an elastic around the back to keep it on  the ground  like glasses  exactly great idea but kids have been  really great about wearing a mask i  gotta be honest better than many many  adults we're all adjusting to it lisa if  a child is feeling nervous what do you  think parents should do to make them  feel a little better  well always it is the case that kids  take their cues from us about what they  should be nervous about and how nervous  they should be so if they're nervous  about wearing a mask we want to be  really matter of fact about it and just  say you know you're going to wear a mask  so that you feel safe at school and when  you feel safe then you can focus on your  learning and focus on enjoying your  friends  and if they're nervous about going back  to school what we can remember is that  kids are always tense during back to  school time there you know is always a  lot of change change brings about stress  they've got new teachers they've got new  classmates they've got new routines and  so the best thing we can do is to help  them express all of their worries and  then to reassure them to say as soon as  you get into the swing of things this  will all make sense your questions will  be answered and you will feel better hey  lisa i just have one follow-up for you i  mean kids pick up on disagreements among  adults and so they know that there is a  certain percentage of adults who are  saying don't wear a mask and they don't  want their kids to get vaccinated they  don't want their kids to follow the  guidelines what do you say to kids who  are picking up on that and wondering  what's going on here  i think we can say a couple of things  one is that we can say this is what we  are learning from physicians about how  best to keep you safe and we're going to  follow that guidance the other thing i  think we should do is to prepare kids  who go to schools where masks are  optional to prepare them for the  possibility that other kids may ask  questions  and here we can give them a few options  to use depending on the situation they  might say i just feel more comfortable  wearing a mask or we can bear in mind  it's a lot to ask a kid to wear a mask  all day and defend that choice and so we  can tell them just kind of cut them a  break we can say to them feel free to  tell your friends you know yeah it's a  drag it's something my parents are  making me do yeah tara this is this is  not the first year sadly that we've been  sending kids to school in a pandemic  what have doctors learned about keeping  kids safe in the classroom well we've  learned so much since march of 2020 when  all of this started i think one big  thing is that schools are not really the  primary drivers of outbreaks schools are  more of the barometer of what's going on  in the community in terms of  transmission but the second big lesson  is layering layering preventive measures  works it's effective and by that i mean  vaccinating kids who are eligible and  all the adults around the kids teachers  staff parents masking as we've talked  about physical distance so three feet or  more proper ventilation or good  ventilation in schools is key and then  we talked about this last week but rapid  frequent testing both of kids who have  symptoms but also asymptomatic  particularly if there's high levels of  transmission you want to be testing like  once a week in schools we've seen this  work in universities in the past year  and then finally keeping kids home when  they're sick and schools really need to  be flexible with their policies looking  at what's happening in community what  the science says and adapting their  policies accordingly and you know now  the cases with kids are going up you  know when this first started you didn't  have to worry so much about the kids now  you do what concerns you most about that  why is that happening absolutely well  we're at this inflection point where  kids are going back to school we have  delta which is so much more contagious  um and really we have so many kids who  still remain unvaccinated so it's not  surprising that we're going to see this  increase in hospitalizations but what's  concerning is that this rise in  infections means that we have kids who  are now at risk potentially from missed  c kids who may develop long covid we  have that's missing what is that the  multisystem inflammatory condition that  we've seen in over about 4 000 kids in  this country  develop we also have concern for the  fact that more variants can develop as  the virus mutates because it's spreading  kids can transmit to others and then  lastly we are overwhelming or have the  potential to overwhelm our hospital  system there have been so many reports  we've talked about of pediatric icus in  this country being filled up how did we  get to this point there was an article i  read last night by two pediatricians who  said listen our kids have sacrificed so  much for us we need to step up as the  adults and prioritize their health and  do the right thing yeah lisa and tara  you guys are great individually together  this has been terrific i think really  valuable information thank you both very  much

